            <ArticleTitle>[Myocardial infarct and coronary fistula. A rare combination].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>511-5, 477</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report an unusual case of a 39-years-old male patient, with previous inferior and anterior non Q wave myocardial infarctions, in whom the coronary arteriography showed an arteriovenous fistula between the left anterior descending coronary artery and the pulmonary artery, without any other coronary lesions. We make a revision of this disease and discuss the mechanisms of ischemia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
